<h2 id="why">Why It Happened Now</h2>
<p>Robinson had been holding in at Falcons camp — present at the facility but not practicing — while he and Atlanta finalized the deal, part of a standoff with Detroit's Jahmyr Gibbs where both backs were reportedly waiting to see who would move first. Robinson's side got there first. He led the NFL with 2,298 yards from scrimmage in 2025, finished fourth in the league with 1,478 rushing yards, and added 820 receiving yards as a true dual-threat weapon — production a panel of AP Pro Football Writers ranked as the league's best at the position heading into 2026.</p>
<p>The deal is also part of a broader pattern in Atlanta this offseason: the Falcons have now extended Robinson, receiver Drake London, tight end Kyle Pitts, and guard Matthew Bergeron, committing to the core of their offense regardless of who ends up starting at quarterback.</p>
<div class="callout">
<strong>Refresher on the Athletic Profile</strong>
  Robinson's combine numbers — a 4.46-second 40, 37-inch vertical, and 10-foot-4 broad jump at 215 pounds — were part of what made him a top-five prospect in 2023. Read the full breakdown in our <a class="inline" href="/bijan-robinson-jahmyr-gibbs-contract-holdout-athletic-profile">Robinson vs. Gibbs athletic comparison</a>.
</div>
<h2 id="gibbs">What It Means for Jahmyr Gibbs</h2>
<p>Robinson's deal effectively sets the new running back market, and Gibbs is next in line. Detroit had been waiting to see how Atlanta's negotiation resolved, and with Robinson now sitting at $22.25 million per year, that becomes the number Gibbs and the Lions are working against — along with Colts running back Jonathan Taylor, who is reportedly also eyeing an extension. Don't be surprised if Gibbs's deal lands close to or above Robinson's within the next few weeks.</p>
